<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mwambutsa IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mwambutsa IV Bangiricenge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mwambutsa IV was mwami (king) of Burundi from 1915 to 1966, reigning under Belgian rule and into independence in 1962. Burundi became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, but ethnic and political violence, including the assassination of prime ministers, undermined his authority. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1965 and was deposed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-v">Ntare V</a> in 1966, shortly before the monarchy was abolished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv.jpg" alt="Mwambutsa IV" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fritz Cohen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mwambutsa_1962.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Prince Bangiricenge at Nyabiyogi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Buyenzi, he was one of two sons of Mwami Mutaga IV and Ngenzahayo and, like other Burundian kings, an ethnic Ganwa. He became king as a child on 16 December 1915, after his father died in a family dispute, and a regency was formed in which family members including Queen Mother Ririkumutima served; a full regency council followed in 1925 with Belgian approval. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> had taken Burundi from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> in 1916. He attended a primary school opened for him at Muramvya, but never converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He was declared of age on 28 August 1929, and on 24 December 1930 he married Thérèse Kanyonga, a Catholic Tutsi of the Abasine clan. The Belgians kept the monarchy under their policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, and Mwambutsa held aloof from party politics, declaring on 8 February 1960 that he belonged to no party. Burundi became independent as a kingdom on 1 July 1962, with Mwambutsa as head of state with wide powers. While the monarchy of Rwanda had been overthrown, he tried to balance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi by choosing prime ministers from each group in turn.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> officers attempted a coup in October 1965, he went into exile and eventually settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In March 1966 he delegated his powers to his only surviving son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-v-of-burundi">Charles Ndizeye</a>, who was brought to power as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-v">Ntare V</a> by a coup on 8 July 1966; a further coup in November abolished the monarchy and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a> to power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mwambutsa spent the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and died there in 1977. His remains were exhumed in 2012 so that they could be taken home for a state funeral in Burundi, but after a legal battle they were reburied in Switzerland in 2016, in accordance with his family's wishes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mwambutsa IV of Burundi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Louis Rwagasore, eldest son of <b>Mwambutsa IV</b>, who led the nationalist party UPRONA to victory in 1961, is honoured as the hero of independence, and the anniversary of his death, 13 October, is a public holiday.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Muramvya, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.25,29.6&amp;year=1954" aria-label="Muramvya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5372 2285 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mwambutsa IV"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5822 2555h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5822 2555h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.25,29.6&amp;year=1954">What was here in 1954? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
